{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A clean categorical axis (printer, hardware, software, network, database) for grouping and prioritizing ticket types. Samples map soundly from short_description ('database', 'network', 'printer'). Strong new grouping dimension."
    },
    {
      "name": "device_component",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Extracts the affected device from text, enabling component-level failure analysis. Values like 'printer', 'monitor', 'server' are well grounded; 'Unknown'/NULL correctly used when text is silent (e.g. connectivity/database rows). Adds a distinct axis from category."
    },
    {
      "name": "problem_type",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Categorizes the failure mode (malfunction, not_working, connectivity_issue, etc.), useful for symptom analysis. Grounded in phrases like 'not working properly' and 'connectivity lost'. Some overlap between 'malfunction' and 'not_working' is fuzzy but still analytically usable."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_action",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Would be useful for resolution-mix analysis, but the source samples (short_description only) describe problems, not resolutions. Values like 'replacement_or_repair' are inferred defaults not stated in the text, and the distribution is heavily skewed (389/500), suggesting speculative labeling rather than grounded extraction."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurring_pattern",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
      "source_groundedness": 0.3,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Recurrence would require cross-row/temporal evidence, but the boolean appears asserted per-row with no textual support (single short_description cannot establish recurrence). 'True' assigned to a single printer ticket is unsupported. Concept is analytically interesting but values look invented."
    },
    {
      "name": "urgency_signal",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.55,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "A bare urgency flag is somewhat generic and partly redundant with the existing 'priority' column. Grounding is weak: 'connectivity lost on floor 3' plausibly urgent, but many assignments are not clearly warranted by the terse text, and true/false split looks partly heuristic."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.72,
  "source_groundedness": 0.68,
  "rationale": "The categorical extraction columns (incident_category, device_component, problem_type) are strong: they give genuine new axes for grouping and are soundly derived from the short_description text, with appropriate Unknown/NULL for silent rows. The three inferential columns (resolution_action, recurring_pattern, urgency_signal) are weaker on groundedness because the available source text describes problems, not resolutions, recurrence, or explicit urgency; their values appear to rely on speculative defaults, and urgency_signal partly duplicates the original priority field. Overall the set provides real analytical leverage but includes several under-grounded inferred columns."
}
